1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,739
Your brain runs on about twenty watts, roughly the power of a dim light bulb.

2
00:00:04,789 --> 00:00:07,973
A single A.I. accelerator draws about seven hundred.

3
00:00:08,023 --> 00:00:10,586
So how does a machine this frugal compute?

4
00:00:10,636 --> 00:00:13,260
Let's open it up the way an engineer would.

5
00:00:13,300 --> 00:00:18,419
Imagine you are handed a bag with eighty-six billion parts that all look alike,

6
00:00:18,469 --> 00:00:21,298
and asked how a computer is built from them.

7
00:00:21,348 --> 00:00:23,916
An engineer would not study their shape.

8
00:00:23,966 --> 00:00:26,402
An engineer would ask three questions.

9
00:00:26,452 --> 00:00:28,562
How many inputs does a part have?

10
00:00:28,612 --> 00:00:29,674
How many outputs?

11
00:00:29,724 --> 00:00:31,310
And what does it put out?

12
00:00:31,333 --> 00:00:34,757
The part is the neuron, and it is surprisingly simple.

13
00:00:34,807 --> 00:00:36,494
Thousands of wires come in.

14
00:00:36,544 --> 00:00:39,518
One wire goes out, but it branches like a tree,

15
00:00:39,568 --> 00:00:42,735
so the same signal reaches thousands of listeners.

16
00:00:42,785 --> 00:00:45,309
Inside, the neuron adds up what arrives.

17
00:00:45,359 --> 00:00:48,461
Some inputs push the sum up, others push it down.

18
00:00:48,511 --> 00:00:50,585
When the sum crosses a threshold,

19
00:00:50,635 --> 00:00:54,252
the neuron fires a short click, about a millisecond long.

20
00:00:54,302 --> 00:00:55,796
Every click is the same,

21
00:00:55,846 --> 00:01:00,943
so everything a neuron says is written in timing: how often it clicks, and when.

22
00:01:00,967 --> 00:01:04,209
Where the wire meets a neighbour there is a tiny gap,

23
00:01:04,259 --> 00:01:06,693
and chemistry carries the signal across.

24
00:01:06,743 --> 00:01:10,544
The wire releases a transmitter, and the neighbour catches it.

25
00:01:10,594 --> 00:01:11,973
Here is the lucky part.

26
00:01:12,023 --> 00:01:16,134
Almost every neuron releases the same substance at all of its tips.

27
00:01:16,184 --> 00:01:19,364
So we can sort the bag by what each neuron releases,

28
00:01:19,414 --> 00:01:23,339
and name each type by the first four letters of its transmitter.

29
00:01:23,389 --> 00:01:25,016
Glutamate neurons are GLUT.

30
00:01:25,066 --> 00:01:26,817
Then GABA, DOPA for dopamine,

31
00:01:26,867 --> 00:01:31,227
SERO for serotonin, NORA for noradrenaline, and ACET for acetylcholine.

32
00:01:31,267 --> 00:01:33,068
Sorted, the bag is lopsided.

33
00:01:33,118 --> 00:01:37,628
Out of every thousand neurons, about nine hundred and sixty are GLUT.

34
00:01:37,678 --> 00:01:40,602
They push the receiver toward firing: a plus.

35
00:01:40,652 --> 00:01:42,915
About thirty-six are GABA: a minus.

36
00:01:42,965 --> 00:01:47,145
Everything else together is a few neurons in a hundred thousand.

37
00:01:47,195 --> 00:01:48,467
A drop in the ocean.

38
00:01:48,500 --> 00:01:51,375
But that drop is wired completely differently.

39
00:01:51,425 --> 00:01:56,972
GLUT and GABA neurons are like telephone lines: each wire goes to particular neighbours.

40
00:01:57,022 --> 00:02:00,851
The tiny DOPA, SERO, NORA and ACET groups are radio stations.

41
00:02:00,901 --> 00:02:03,331
Their wires spread across huge regions,

42
00:02:03,381 --> 00:02:06,638
and millions of cells hear the same message at once.

43
00:02:06,688 --> 00:02:08,864
The telephone network carries data.

44
00:02:08,914 --> 00:02:12,107
The radio carries settings: how loud everything is,

45
00:02:12,157 --> 00:02:15,032
whether to learn, whether it is time to sleep.

46
00:02:15,082 --> 00:02:18,784
Every computer is built this way: billions of memory cells,

47
00:02:18,834 --> 00:02:21,010
and a handful of control registers.

48
00:02:21,033 --> 00:02:21,773
One subtlety.

49
00:02:21,823 --> 00:02:25,356
The molecule does not know whether it is a plus or a minus.

50
00:02:25,406 --> 00:02:26,084
It is a key.

51
00:02:26,134 --> 00:02:30,457
What happens is decided by the lock, the receptor on the receiving cell.

52
00:02:30,507 --> 00:02:35,923
The same dopamine excites one cell and quiets another, depending on which lock it carries.

53
00:02:35,967 --> 00:02:38,448
The most famous radio channel is dopamine.

54
00:02:38,498 --> 00:02:39,834
What does it broadcast?

55
00:02:39,884 --> 00:02:44,354
Give an animal an unexpected drop of juice, and its dopamine neurons burst.

56
00:02:44,404 --> 00:02:46,523
Now light a lamp before every juice.

57
00:02:46,573 --> 00:02:51,345
The animal learns that the lamp predicts juice, and the burst moves to the lamp.

58
00:02:51,395 --> 00:02:54,056
And if the lamp comes but the juice does not,

59
00:02:54,106 --> 00:02:57,913
the dopamine neurons fall silent exactly when the juice was due.

60
00:02:57,963 --> 00:02:59,540
Dopamine does not say good.

61
00:02:59,590 --> 00:03:05,627
It says better than I expected: the same error signal that programs use to learn from their mistakes.

62
00:03:05,667 --> 00:03:10,964
So the brain is a vast network of near-identical parts: pluses and minuses for data,

63
00:03:11,014 --> 00:03:14,337
and a few radio stations that tune the whole machine.

64
00:03:14,387 --> 00:03:17,392
Next time: what you can build from pluses alone.

65
00:03:17,442 --> 00:03:19,238
The full book, with the math,

66
00:03:19,288 --> 00:03:23,693
the models and the exercises, is free and available in many languages.

67
